PUT-YOUR-HOSTNAME-HERE

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS FILE IS NOT LIVE. Copy it to `CNAME` (no extension) and replace the first line with the
# bare hostname, then delete everything below it: GitHub Pages reads the whole file as one
# hostname, so a real CNAME contains exactly one line and no comments.
#
# Owner direction: "a real domain, not a github.io path" / "a nice backrooms url".
#
# Why this is an example rather than the real thing: **no file in this repository may name a
# hostname the deploy does not serve.** A CNAME naming a domain nobody owns yet does not fail
# loudly - it silently breaks the site, because Pages stops answering on github.io and starts
# waiting for DNS that will never arrive. So the shape is authored, ready, and inert.
#
# WHAT TO DO WHEN THE DOMAIN EXISTS
#
#   1. Register the domain.
#   2. At the DNS host, for an apex domain (example.com) create four A records pointing at
#      185.199.108.153, 185.199.109.153, 185.199.110.153 and 185.199.111.153 - and, if the host
#      supports it, the matching AAAA records. For a subdomain (wiki.example.com) create one
#      CNAME record pointing at <owner>.github.io instead.
#   3. `cp docs/CNAME.example docs/CNAME`, put the bare hostname on line one, delete the rest.
#   4. In the repository's Settings -> Pages, set the custom domain to the same hostname and
#      tick "Enforce HTTPS" once the certificate has been issued.
#   5. Nothing in the wiki needs editing. No page hard-codes the site's own address; links within
#      the site go through Jekyll's `relative_url`, so they follow whatever domain serves them.
#
# Verify with `curl -sI https://<hostname>` returning 200 and a `github.com` server header
# before announcing it anywhere.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
